  steps: [
    { math: '1 · stimulus', note: 'The seed the piece grows from — the syllabus\'s devising process begins with "the interpretation of a stimulus from which learners develop a scenario and its characters". A poem, a photograph, an issue of conscience, a piece of music: the stimulus is explored, not illustrated.' },
    { math: '2 · dramatic intention', note: 'Devising demands "establishing a clear dramatic intention" — what you want the audience to understand or feel by the end. Every later choice (structure, dialogue, blocking) is tested against this one sentence.' },
    { math: '3 · exposition', note: 'The scene opens out its world: who these people are, where we are, what is at stake. Good exposition hides inside action and dialogue; clumsy exposition announces itself as information the characters already know.' },
    { math: '4 · obstacle', note: 'Conflict enters and tension begins to climb. The obstacle is what stops a character getting what they want — and pacing is your control on it: short exchanges, interruptions and rising rhythm tighten the spring; pause and silence let the audience wait.' },
    { math: '5 · climax', note: 'Peak tension and emotional intensity — the moment the intention lands or is reversed. Contrast and dynamics do the work here: loud against quiet, crowded against empty, fast against frozen, so the peak reads as a peak.' },
    { math: '6 · resolution', note: 'The fall: what has changed, and what it means. Exposition, climax and resolution are the syllabus\'s own core terms — the skeleton of structure and plot on which characterisation, dialogue and physicality hang.' },
  ],
};

